# 2. WHAT DOES "CRACKING WI-FI" MEAN?

In a security-testing context, Wi-Fi cracking generally means attempting to recover or validate a wireless network credential or security weakness from captured wireless information.

It is different from simply connecting to a Wi-Fi network.

Example:

```text
Wireless AP
     ↓
Capture authorized traffic
     ↓
Analyze protocol
     ↓
Attempt credential recovery
     ↓
Validate security
     ↓
Report weakness
```

---

# 3. WEP VS WPA/WPA2

| Feature | WEP | WPA/WPA2 |
|---|---|---|
| Security generation | Obsolete | Newer |
| Main weakness | Weak cryptographic design | Depends heavily on credential/security configuration |
| IV handling | Major weakness | Different key-management design |
| Modern security | Insecure | WPA2-AES remains widely used |
| Password recovery | Can exploit statistical IV weaknesses | WPA/WPA2-Personal often involves offline password guessing |
| Recommendation | Never use | Prefer WPA3; WPA2-AES if required |

